{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 26, :comments ["<b>And there remained two men, etc.</b> One needs to know: (a) the intent of the statement ״וַיִּשָּׁאֲרוּ״ — from where did they remain? (b) Why was it necessary to mention their names? (c) His saying <b>and rested upon them</b>, etc. — whichever way you look at it: if they are among the seventy, behold the verse has already included them in the general body of the seventy, for it is written ״וַיָּאצֵל etc. וַיִּתֵּן עַל שִׁבְעִים״, whereas his saying ״וַתָּנַח״ implies something new that had not been mentioned until now; and if they are not among the seventy, did not HaShem say to Moses only seventy? (d) His saying <b>and they were among those written</b> — the intent of this is not known. (e) His saying <b>and they did not go out</b> — behold, from his saying ״וַיִּשָּׁאֲרוּ בַּמַּחֲנֶה״ it is self-evident that they did not go out. (f) His saying <b>and they prophesied</b> at the end — whereas it should have juxtaposed what followed upon them in its own right and what was done from them in its own right, in this order: ״וַיִּשָּׁאֲרוּ שְׁנֵי אֲנָשִׁים etc. וְהֵמָּה בַּכְּתֻבִים וְלֹא יָצְאוּ הָאֹהֱלָה וַתָּנַח עֲלֵיהֶם הָרוּחַ וַיִּתְנַבְּאוּ״ — so why did he write the matters not in the proper sequence?" "<b>And our Sages</b> disagreed (Sifrei Numbers 95:) about from where they remained. Some say they remained from the seventy and two that Moses selected from Israel — six from each tribe equally — and he drew lots to bring out the two that exceeded the seventy that HaShem had told him, and these are the two who remained. And Rabbi Shimon said: they were among the seventy, but they remained because they did not wish to enter the Tent of Meeting out of humility and lowliness, and they stayed in the camp — end. According to the opinion that says they remained from among the seventy, the verse may be explained in this manner: ״וַיִּשָּׁאֲרוּ שְׁנֵי אֲנָשִׁים בַּמַּחֲנֶה״ — because they considered themselves unworthy. <b>The name of the one</b>, etc. — meaning, they were known for upright qualities and held in esteem in the eyes of Israel; it was for this reason that they remained in the camp, for otherwise the people would have reason to murmur against them — that they had despised, Heaven forbid, the resting of the Spirit upon them, and they would be found profaning HaShem's name and the Most High would reject them for this reason. But because the name of the one was the distinguished name Eldad and the name of the second was Medad, therefore the people did not judge their action negatively, and for this they were not rejected. <b>And the Spirit rested upon them</b> — the very same [Spirit] that had rested upon the seventy; the distance between the Tent of Meeting and the camp did not prevent it, and it rested also upon them. And he was precise in saying <b>וַתָּנַח</b>, to say that there the Spirit found its place — as they said, that the rest of the seventy ceased [prophesying] but these did not cease from prophecy. And before mentioning the prophecy he mentioned matters through which the people would validate that words of prophecy in their mouths were true, and he said <b>and they were among those written</b>, meaning, from those seventy who were written. And so that you not think that the written ones are those that were written on the lots' ballots, therefore he said ״וְלֹא יָצְאוּ הָאֹהֱלָה״ — from this you learn that they could have gone out. And through this their words were authenticated as being prophecy, and that is his saying <b>and they prophesied.</b> And without this mentioned condition, who would uphold their words as words of prophecy? And through this all the fine points are resolved." "<b>And according to the words of</b> the one who says [they remained] from the seventy and two, the verse may be explained in this manner: <b>וַיִּשָּׁאֲרוּ</b> — meaning, even though the two men who exceeded the seventy remained in the camp, <b>the name of the one</b>, etc., even so, ״וַתָּנַח עֲלֵיהֶם הָרוּחַ״ as well, in addition to what rested upon the seventy as he said above, ״וַיָּאצֶל מִן הָרוּחַ etc. וַיִּתֵּן עַל שִׁבְעִים אִישׁ״. And one must examine rightly why it was necessary to say ״וַתָּנַח עֲלֵיהֶם הָרוּחַ״: since in the first verse he mentioned that He gave the Spirit only upon the seventy, HaShem informed you that it rested also upon the two. And he said <b>and they were among those written</b>, etc. — this comes to give a reason to validate their prophecy as I explained above, and he said ״וְהֵמָּה בַּכְּתֻבִים״ — meaning, among the ballots that Moses wrote, seventy-two elders. From now on they are indeed worthy of prophecy like the rest of the seventy, and it is possible that they would prophesy. And so that you not understand that they were among the written ones who came to the Tent, therefore the verse clarified and said ״וְלֹא יָצְאוּ״ — among those summoned in the ballots to come to the Tent." "<b>And by way of</b> derash, perhaps the verse informs us that the two who did not go out with the ballots were ashamed and entered into the camp — something that none of Israel did, for all of them went out to the Tent of Meeting, while the two hid in the camp. And HaShem saw their shame and their humiliation, ״וַיִּתְנַבְּאוּ״, and that is his saying <b>and they were among those written</b> — which are the ballots, and for this reason ״וְלֹא יָצְאוּ״ from the camp; and HaShem showed them favor and fulfilled their desire, ״וַיִּתְנַבְּאוּ״."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:08:08.720815579Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}